Currying sends a map out of a product to a map from the first factor into the function space of the other two. An input neighborhood relates to a function-space neighborhood when the section of the map at that input lies in the function-space neighborhood. The master input relates to the master of the function space. The right intersection of two such neighborhoods is their intersection in the function space. Monotonicity uses that a smaller input gives a larger section and that function-space neighborhoods are upward closed.
